<article class="env-remark" id="06NL">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06NL">Remark <span data-tag="06NL">9.5.1.7</span> <span class="named">(Sizes of Presentable $\infty $-Categories)</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a presentable $\infty $-category. We then have two possibilities: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
The $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is equivalent to the nerve of a partially ordered set $Q$. In this case, Example <a data-tag="06NK" href="/tag/06NK">9.5.1.5</a> shows that $Q$ is small, so that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is essentially small. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is not equivalent to the nerve of a partially ordered set. In this case, Proposition <a data-tag="048B" href="/tag/048B">7.1.2.15</a> shows that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ cannot be essentially small. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
